          "english_quote": "on what grounds Moses had allowed seven to marry",
          "issue": "The draft defensibly treats ἑπτά as the subject of γαμεῖν, but the indeclinable numeral can also be its object: permission to marry seven. That alternative materially changes the marriage question. The note's statement that no object of “marry” is supplied describes the chosen analysis, not an unambiguous property of the Greek, and the alternative is not preserved.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the current main rendering only with an expanded note identifying the subject/object ambiguity: “ἑπτά is taken here as the infinitive's subject, ‘allowed seven [people] to marry,’ a contextual reading of the Sadducees' question. It could instead be the object, ‘allowed [someone] to marry seven [people].’ The wording does not explicitly identify the seven as brothers, men, or women. Under the chosen subject reading, the object of ‘marry’ remains unstated.” Do not import a spouse or other narrative details into the main text. The revised disclosure requires fresh review; no print verification or emendation is needed for this ambiguity.",
